================================================================================
Attribution for bundled unit data
================================================================================

The file `resources/commensura/units.edn` is generated (by the dev-only tool
`commensura.units.convert`, in `dev/`) from Frink's `units.txt` data file, and is
a derivative work of it.

Data lineage:

  * Frink and its `units.txt` data file
    Alan Eliasen <eliasen@mindspring.com>  —  https://frinklang.org/
    (source: dev-resources/frink/units.txt)

  * GNU units
    Adrian Mariano <adrianm@gnu.org>
    Frink's data file is "adapted, modified, and extended from the units
    database for use with GNU units."  GNU units is licensed under the GNU
    General Public License; this lineage is why commensura is distributed under
    the GPL.

  * The Bell Labs UNIX `units` program (Version 7, 1979), from which the GNU
    units program and its interface descend.

Primary sources of the underlying values, as credited in the `units.txt` header,
include (non-exhaustive):

  * NIST Special Publication 811 (1995)
  * CODATA Recommended Values of the Fundamental Physical Constants
  * BIPM SI Brochure
  * CRC Handbook of Chemistry and Physics (70th ed.)
  * Oxford English Dictionary; and numerous works on historical weights and
    measures (see the header of dev-resources/frink/units.txt for the full list).

No claim of copyright is made over the underlying factual measurement data; this
